# Secrets — never commit, even though this project has none of its own.
# The rule stands regardless: a stray file does not care that the kit is secret-free.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
secrets/
credentials.json
.netrc

# Build and dependency output.
# NOTE: none of this should ever exist here — ADR-002 forbids a runtime, and FF-009
# fails the build if a package manifest or lockfile enters the published payload.
# These entries are a tripwire, not an expectation: if git starts ignoring files
# matched below, something has been added that supersedes an accepted ADR.
node_modules/
dist/
build/
out/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/

# Test and CI artifacts. The plans and fixtures ARE committed; run output is not.
coverage/
.coverage
*.log
03-tests/05-executable/**/tmp/
03-tests/05-executable/**/output/

# Editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~

# =============================================================================
# NOT IGNORED, DELIBERATELY
# =============================================================================
#
# spec/                    The specification IS the source of truth. Committed, always.
#                          A git history of specification changes is the closest thing to
#                          observability this project can honestly have — every other
#                          signal is forbidden by CON-007.
#
# 03-tests/.../fixtures/   Answer scripts and golden workspaces are fixtures, not output.
#                          They are how a question change is proved to have helped.
#
# .env.example             Empty by design, with the reasons. Explicitly un-ignored above.
#
# The same rule is a product requirement: REQ-F-035 keeps a DEVELOPER'S generated spec/
# folder committed too — and the kit must never add an ignore rule for it in their
# .gitignore, or modify that file at all (CON-005).
